<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samsat</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Samosata</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samsat is a town in Adıyaman province in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, on the site of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samosata">Samosata</a> on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. It was the capital of the Neo-Hittite kingdom of Kummuh and later of the Hellenistic kingdom of Commagene, and the birthplace of the satirist Lucian. The ancient city mound was flooded by the reservoir of the Atatürk Dam in the late 1980s, and the town was rebuilt nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samsat.jpg" alt="Samsat" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Radosław Botev · CC BY 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samsat_Turkey.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
